#N canvas 20 20 1700 1240 12;
#X text 20 20 t_sysexin -- a stand-in for [sysexin] that a DRIVER CAN DRIVE. Every gate that makes a scratch copy rewrites [sysexin] to [t_sysexin] there \; nothing in Cut It/ is touched and this file is never deployed., f 84;
#X text 20 130 ⛔ IT EXISTS BECAUSE PRESENCE HAS NO OTHER ORACLE. The whole hot-swap model turns on a device-inquiry REPLY \, and a reply arrives on [sysexin] -- a MIDI input \, so there is no bus in front of it and no way to fake one. Without this stub the never-present case that item 235 is about could only ever be tested by unplugging a real cable. test/gate/lib-scratch.sh listed sysexin under MIDI_INVENTORY as the last class with no stub \, precisely because nothing needed to drive it yet., f 84;
#X text 20 310 ⛔ TWO OUTLETS \, AND THAT IS MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSUMED. Pd 0.49's [sysexin] is byte then PORT \, verified by connecting each outlet in turn in a throwaway patch: outlet 1 loads in silence and outlet 2 fails with connection failed. ⚠️ THE PROBE WAS FALSIFIED BEFORE IT WAS BELIEVED -- outlet 5 was tried first and DID report connection failed \, so the silence at outlet 1 means the outlet exists rather than meaning Pd says nothing either way. A stub with one outlet would silently drop every cord the patch hangs off the port., f 84;
#X text 20 510 creation args: none \, and none are needed -- [sysexin] takes none. inlets: none -- it is a SOURCE. outlets 2: byte \, then port \, exactly as the real object. ⚠️ THE MESSAGE IS PORT FIRST AND THE BYTES AFTER IT \, which is the opposite of the outlet order on purpose: a driver names the port once and then lists a whole frame \, \; t-sysexin 1 240 126 0 6 2 0 32 41 247., f 84;
#X text 20 670 ⚠️ EACH BYTE EMITS ITS PORT FIRST \, THEN ITSELF \, which is what the real object does -- Pd fires outlets right to left \, so a patch that stores the port in a cold inlet always has the current one before the byte arrives. Reproducing that here rather than emitting the port once is what lets a gate assert a port gate at all., f 84;
#X text 20 810 ⚠️ t-sysexin IS A BARE GLOBAL NAME OUTSIDE THE ALLOWLIST \, and that is deliberate and safe: it exists ONLY in the gate's scratch copy \, exactly like t-ctlin and t-notein \, and Cut It/ never contains it. State it \, so nobody fixes it., f 84;
#X text 20 940 ⚠️ THE WHOLE FRAME LANDS IN ONE LOGICAL INSTANT \, because [until] does not advance the clock. That is correct for anything COUNTING bytes and wrong for anything TIMING them -- so do not build a gate that measures the gap between two bytes of one frame. On the wire they are microseconds apart anyway., f 84;
#X obj 20 1060 r t-sysexin;
#X obj 20 1110 list split 1;
#X obj 400 1170 t l l;
